Z||000003||bHooper||10-25-2000||07:08 AM||||I will have to agree with Doug.  I have used the stuff b4, 7 or 8 years ago.  It does work well on cars that have been washed several days b4.<P>The problem that I have is if the car is real dusty, dirt, whatever... you will scratch the paint using this stuff.<P><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000004||fropuff||10-25-2000||09:11 AM||||Yeah, I thought about that.  When I got some of it, I was going to wash my car about a day before to get all the water dried off.  Then I was going to use it like normal.  This seems a little stupid on my part; I might as well just get some good wax.  Anybody know of some that will get rid of my paint swirls, streaks, etc.?  I was thinking of a three-step like Mcguires, but I'm not quite sure.  I just want to get rid of the streaks and stuff like that.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fropuff||10-25-2000||09:13 AM||||I wonder if I can get a trial bottle anywhere.  There's a dealer here in Minnesota, but it's like $40 for a large bottle and spray bottle.  I wonder.....||||reg||1
Z||000006||Oreif||10-25-2000||11:42 AM||||Using the Meguire's 3-step will remove the swirls and fine scratches. When you are done with the 3-step the paint will shine like glass. If you have some deeper scratches or mar's, use the Meguire's "Fine Cut Cleaner" It comes in a tan bottle. The  F.C.C. will also remove some light fading in the clear coat too. My car is almost 90% original 15 year old paint. (nose and scoop were painted) and it shines like glass! (Although black cars are hard to keep clean since they show everything!) Just to give an example how shiny it gets, look at the roof on the pic below, Yes I have a sunroof. Look closely and you'll see the outline and hinge caps.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DSC00007_1.jpg">  <P><BR>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 10-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||TheFiero64||10-25-2000||11:52 AM||||I love the look of a clean and shinny black Fiero. I don't have the patience for black paint. I am happy with my red.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||6
Z||000008||Tigger||10-25-2000||01:23 PM||||I'm sure the dri wash works, but I'd only do it after driving to a show and the car was pretty clean to begin with.  <P>To give a Fiero a good bath probably takes about the same amount of time for a dry wash anyway.  Plus if your driving it everyday, it's going to have a build up of road dust that will create fine scratches in the finish if you try to wipe over it dirty no matter how well the dry wash works.<P>Orief has a good system, the 3 stage Meguires works great.  After a weekend of wheel'in and rubb'in you'll want to keep it shiny.  Best thing, go out and spend a few bucks (about $10 to $15) on a "real" shammois (shammy?). (sp?)  I use one on my car, and will never go back to towels.  After they break in, they work great.  Hang the shammy up to dry in a clean place and it'll be ready the next time and last a long time.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tiggerblue.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||DaRkLoRD||10-25-2000||03:00 PM||||Has anyone used a product called "HydroWax"?<P>I saw a demo of this a couple of years ago and it was amazing... I'm just not sure how it'd work on the plastic body panels, if it'd be any different than on metal etc.<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
